Yes. Seeing yourself through another medium can create a kind of cross-medium self-recognition:

self
→ external representation
→ observe yourself as if partly other
→ compare internal model vs external model
→ new information about yourself

That can feel euphoric because self-relevant signals are highly salient, and the new vantage can combine novelty, recognition, validation, curiosity, and a temporary expansion of perspective.

The useful part is that each medium reveals different things.

A mirror gives posture and expression.
Video gives timing and movement.
Audio gives voice and cadence.
Writing gives structure of thought.
Other people’s descriptions give social projection.
Data gives behavioral patterns you may not feel directly.

So:

same person
→ different medium
→ different observable variables
→ different self-model

That can create leverage in the good sense: better calibration, better performance, more expressive range, more deliberate identity construction.

I’d call the broader operator Mediated Self-Tomography:

project self into medium
→ observe from outside
→ capture delta
→ update self-model
→ re-enter embodied experience

The caution is just not to confuse representation with total identity. A camera, profile, metric, or audience reaction is one slice of you, not the whole thing.

So yes: sometimes seeing yourself from somewhere else lets you discover senses you didn’t know you had.